//? Title: Process capability indices
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare a process's spread and centring with its specification limits through Cp, Cpk, Pp, Ppk and Cpm, quantify their sampling uncertainty, and see what between-subgroup drift and non-normality do to them

A capability index compares the width of the specification, \(USL-LSL\), with the natural spread of the process, \(6\sigma\):

- \(C_p=(USL-LSL)/(6\sigma)\) ignores centring;
- \(C_{pk}=\min(USL-\mu,\mu-LSL)/(3\sigma)\) penalizes an off-centre process;
- \(C_{pm}=(USL-LSL)/(6\sqrt{\sigma^2+(\mu-T)^2})\) penalizes distance from a target \(T\).

The C-indices use the *within-subgroup* standard deviation, estimated from subgroup ranges, subgroup SDs or the pooled SD, or from moving ranges for individual data. They describe what the process could do if shifts between subgroups were removed. The P-indices \(P_p\) and \(P_{pk}\) use the *overall* SD of all data and describe what the process actually delivered. A common requirement is \(C_{pk}\ge1.33\), which corresponds to about 63 ppm outside the specification for a centred normal process.

`process_capability` takes subgrouped data (equal or unequal sizes) or individual measurements, and one- or two-sided limits. It returns:

- both SD estimates;
- Cp, Cpl, Cpu and Cpk, Pp, Ppl, Ppu and Ppk, and Cpm, each with a confidence interval (chi-square for Cp, Pp and Cpm; Bissell's approximation for the k-type indices);
- the expected (normal-theory) and observed parts per million outside the limits.

`range_constant_d2` and `sd_constant_c4` give the unbiasing constants; \(d_2\) is computed exactly rather than taken from a table. The tests reproduce `qcc::process.capability` and `qcc::sd.xbar` / `sd.xbar.one` on the piston-ring data. This covers all within-SD estimators, unequal subgroup sizes, moving ranges of span 2 and 3, and one-sided limits. They also check \(d_2\) against numerical integration in R; qcc's own table is rounded to three decimals.

The example is Montgomery's piston-ring inside diameters: 25 subgroups of 5, with specification 74.000 ± 0.05 mm. Three simulations follow, each with 4000 samples:

- the precision of \(\hat C_{pk}\) and the coverage of its interval for a process with true \(C_{pk}=1.33\);
- what between-subgroup drift does to Cpk and Ppk;
- how wrong the normal-theory ppm becomes for skewed data with the same mean and SD.

```{cpp, id=cap, file="figures/process_capability.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/distributions.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/process_capability.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <format>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::WithinSigma;
using Groups = std::vector<std::vector<double>>;

// Piston-ring inside diameters (mm), Montgomery; qcc::pistonrings, samples 1-25.
const Groups rings{
    {74.030, 74.002, 74.019, 73.992, 74.008},
    {73.995, 73.992, 74.001, 74.011, 74.004},
    {73.988, 74.024, 74.021, 74.005, 74.002},
    {74.002, 73.996, 73.993, 74.015, 74.009},
    {73.992, 74.007, 74.015, 73.989, 74.014},
    {74.009, 73.994, 73.997, 73.985, 73.993},
    {73.995, 74.006, 73.994, 74.000, 74.005},
    {73.985, 74.003, 73.993, 74.015, 73.988},
    {74.008, 73.995, 74.009, 74.005, 74.004},
    {73.998, 74.000, 73.990, 74.007, 73.995},
    {73.994, 73.998, 73.994, 73.995, 73.990},
    {74.004, 74.000, 74.007, 74.000, 73.996},
    {73.983, 74.002, 73.998, 73.997, 74.012},
    {74.006, 73.967, 73.994, 74.000, 73.984},
    {74.012, 74.014, 73.998, 73.999, 74.007},
    {74.000, 73.984, 74.005, 73.998, 73.996},
    {73.994, 74.012, 73.986, 74.005, 74.007},
    {74.006, 74.010, 74.018, 74.003, 74.000},
    {73.984, 74.002, 74.003, 74.005, 73.997},
    {74.000, 74.010, 74.013, 74.020, 74.003},
    {73.988, 74.001, 74.009, 74.005, 73.996},
    {74.004, 73.999, 73.990, 74.006, 74.009},
    {74.010, 73.989, 73.990, 74.009, 74.014},
    {74.015, 74.008, 73.993, 74.000, 74.010},
    {73.982, 73.984, 73.995, 74.017, 74.013}};

std::string show(const dm::stats::CapabilityIndex& c) {
    return std::format("{:.3f} [{:.3f}, {:.3f}]", c.value, c.lower, c.upper);
}

// k subgroups of size m: N(0, 1) within, plus N(0, drift^2) subgroup shifts.
Groups draw(dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, std::size_t k, std::size_t m, double drift) {
    Groups g(k);
    for (auto& s : g) {
        const double shift = drift * rng.normal();
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < m; ++i) s.push_back(shift + rng.normal());
    }
    return g;
}

double normal_density(double x, double mean, double sd) {
    const double z = (x - mean) / sd;
    return std::exp(-0.5 * z * z) / (sd * std::sqrt(2 * std::numbers::pi));
}

int main() {
    const dm::stats::CapabilityOptions spec{.lsl = 73.95, .usl = 74.05, .target = 74.0};
    const auto c = dm::stats::process_capability(rings, spec);
    auto within = [&](WithinSigma w) {
        auto o = spec;
        o.within = w;
        return dm::stats::process_capability(rings, o).sigma_within;
    };
    std::println("Mean {:.4f} mm; within SD: pooled {:.5f}, Rbar/d2 {:.5f}, "
                 "Sbar/c4 {:.5f}; overall SD {:.5f}.\n", c.mean, c.sigma_within,
                 within(WithinSigma::average_range), within(WithinSigma::average_sd),
                 c.sigma_overall);
    std::println("| Index | Estimate [95% CI] | Index | Estimate [95% CI] |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |");
    std::println("| Cp | {} | Pp | {} |", show(c.cp), show(c.pp));
    std::println("| Cpl | {} | Ppl | {} |", show(c.cpl), show(c.ppl));
    std::println("| Cpu | {} | Ppu | {} |", show(c.cpu), show(c.ppu));
    std::println("| Cpk | {} | Ppk | {} |", show(c.cpk), show(c.ppk));
    std::println("| Cpm | {} | | |", show(c.cpm));
    std::println("\nExpected ppm outside: {:.2f} (within), {:.2f} (overall); "
                 "observed {:.0f}.\n", c.expected_ppm_within, c.expected_ppm_overall,
                 c.observed_ppm);

    // 1. Precision: true Cpk = 1.33 (mean 0.5, sigma 1, limits 0.5 -+ 3.99).
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(11);
    const int reps = 4000;
    const double lsl = 0.5 - 3.99, usl = 0.5 + 3.99;
    std::println("| Subgroups x size | Mean Cpk | 10%-90% range | Coverage of 95% CI | "
                 "P(estimate >= 1.33) | P(lower limit >= 1.0) |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const auto [k, m] : {std::pair{6uz, 5uz}, {10uz, 5uz}, {25uz, 5uz}, {50uz, 5uz}}) {
        std::vector<double> est;
        double cover = 0, above = 0, lower_ok = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            Groups g = draw(rng, k, m, 0.0);
            for (auto& s : g)
                for (double& v : s) v += 0.5;
            const auto e = dm::stats::process_capability(g, {.lsl = lsl, .usl = usl});
            est.push_back(e.cpk.value);
            cover += e.cpk.lower <= 1.33 && 1.33 <= e.cpk.upper;
            above += e.cpk.value >= 1.33;
            lower_ok += e.cpk.lower >= 1.0;
        }
        std::ranges::sort(est);
        double mean = 0;
        for (const double v : est) mean += v / reps;
        std::println("| {} x {} | {:.3f} | {:.2f}-{:.2f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                     k, m, mean, est[reps / 10], est[9 * reps / 10], cover / reps,
                     above / reps, lower_ok / reps);
    }

    // 2. Drift between subgroups: within SD 1, shift SD tau, limits -+4.
    std::println("\n| Shift SD / within SD | Mean Cpk | Mean Ppk | True long-term Ppk | "
                 "Expected ppm (within) | Expected ppm (overall) | True ppm |");
    std::println("| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (const double tau : {0.0, 0.5, 1.0}) {
        double cpk = 0, ppk = 0, pw = 0, po = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            const auto e = dm::stats::process_capability(draw(rng, 25, 5, tau),
                                                         {.lsl = -4, .usl = 4});
            cpk += e.cpk.value / reps, ppk += e.ppk.value / reps;
            pw += e.expected_ppm_within / reps, po += e.expected_ppm_overall / reps;
        }
        const double s = std::sqrt(1 + tau * tau);
        std::println("| {:.1f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} | {:.1f} |",
                     tau, cpk, ppk, 4 / (3 * s), pw, po,
                     2e6 * dm::random::normal_cdf(-4 / s));
    }

    // 3. Skewed data: lognormal(0, 0.5); USL at mean + 4 SD, so Ppu = 1.33.
    const double sl = 0.5, mu = std::exp(sl * sl / 2);
    const double sd = std::sqrt((std::exp(sl * sl) - 1) * std::exp(sl * sl));
    const double usl_skew = mu + 4 * sd;
    const double true_ppm = 1e6 * dm::random::normal_cdf(-std::log(usl_skew) / sl);
    double ppu = 0, est_ppm = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        Groups g(25);
        for (auto& s : g)
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) s.push_back(std::exp(sl * rng.normal()));
        const auto e = dm::stats::process_capability(g, {.usl = usl_skew});
        ppu += e.ppu.value / reps, est_ppm += e.expected_ppm_overall / reps;
    }
    std::println("\nLognormal process with USL = mean + 4 SD: mean Ppu {:.3f}; mean "
                 "normal-theory ppm {:.1f}; true ppm {:.0f}.", ppu, est_ppm, true_ppm);

    // Figure: histogram of the rings with within and overall normal curves.
    std::vector<double> all;
    for (const auto& s : rings) all.insert(all.end(), s.begin(), s.end());
    const double w = 0.004;
    std::vector<double> mids, dens;
    for (double b = 73.966; b < 74.036; b += w) {
        double cnt = 0;
        for (const double v : all) cnt += v >= b && v < b + w;
        mids.push_back(b + w / 2), dens.push_back(cnt / (all.size() * w));
    }
    std::vector<double> xs, fw, fo;
    for (double x = 73.94; x <= 74.06; x += 0.0005) {
        xs.push_back(x);
        fw.push_back(normal_density(x, c.mean, c.sigma_within));
        fo.push_back(normal_density(x, c.mean, c.sigma_overall));
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.bars(mids, dens, "Piston rings", {170, 190, 210}, w * 0.95)
        .line(xs, fw, "Normal, within SD", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .line(xs, fo, "Normal, overall SD", {176, 72, 43}, 2)
        .abline_v(73.95, {60, 60, 60})
        .abline_v(74.05, {60, 60, 60})
        .abline_v(74.0, {160, 160, 160})
        .text(73.951, 30, "LSL", {60, 60, 60}, 13)
        .text(74.051, 30, "USL", {60, 60, 60}, 13)
        .y_limits(0, 55)
        .title("Piston-ring diameters against the specification")
        .x_label("Inside diameter (mm)")
        .y_label("Density")
        .size(660, 410);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/process_capability.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Mean 74.0012 mm; within SD: pooled 0.00989, Rbar/d2 0.00979, Sbar/c4 0.00983; overall SD 0.01007.

| Index | Estimate [95% CI] | Index | Estimate [95% CI] |
| --- | ---: | --- | ---: |
| Cp | 1.686 [1.476, 1.895] | Pp | 1.655 [1.449, 1.861] |
| Cpl | 1.725 [1.503, 1.948] | Ppl | 1.694 [1.475, 1.913] |
| Cpu | 1.646 [1.433, 1.859] | Ppu | 1.616 [1.407, 1.826] |
| Cpk | 1.646 [1.433, 1.859] | Ppk | 1.616 [1.407, 1.826] |
| Cpm | 1.644 [1.439, 1.849] | | |

Expected ppm outside: 0.51 (within), 0.81 (overall); observed 0.

| Subgroups x size | Mean Cpk | 10%-90% range | Coverage of 95% CI | P(estimate >= 1.33) | P(lower limit >= 1.0) |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 6 x 5 | 1.310 | 1.08-1.57 | 0.926 | 0.407 | 0.328 |
| 10 x 5 | 1.313 | 1.13-1.51 | 0.920 | 0.413 | 0.594 |
| 25 x 5 | 1.311 | 1.19-1.44 | 0.923 | 0.410 | 0.963 |
| 50 x 5 | 1.315 | 1.23-1.40 | 0.921 | 0.404 | 1.000 |

| Shift SD / within SD | Mean Cpk | Mean Ppk | True long-term Ppk | Expected ppm (within) | Expected ppm (overall) | True ppm |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.0 | 1.315 | 1.318 | 1.333 | 110.7 | 97.9 | 63.3 |
| 0.5 | 1.306 | 1.174 | 1.193 | 116.3 | 468.3 | 346.6 |
| 1.0 | 1.282 | 0.919 | 0.943 | 142.4 | 5652.7 | 4677.7 |

Lognormal process with USL = mean + 4 SD: mean Ppu 1.363; mean normal-theory ppm 155.4; true ppm 5651.
\image(figures/process_capability.svg)
// result_end

The piston-ring process is centred within 0.0012 mm of the 74.000 target. Its spread is small relative to the ±0.05 mm tolerance: \(C_{pk}=1.65\), with a 95% interval of 1.43–1.86, well above 1.33. The three within-SD estimators agree to within 1%, and the overall SD is only 2% larger. The process is stable, so \(P_{pk}\approx C_{pk}\), and the expected fraction outside the specification is below one part per million. The histogram sits well inside the limits.

The simulations show three ways these numbers can mislead:

- **Sampling error.** With a true \(C_{pk}\) of 1.33, a study of 30 parts reports anything from 1.08 to 1.57 (10th–90th percentile). The estimate reaches 1.33 only 41% of the time at every sample size, because the minimum of two estimated indices is biased downward. Bissell's interval covers 92–93%, slightly below nominal. To show capability with confidence, the *lower* limit has to clear the requirement. With 30 parts the lower limit clears even 1.0 only a third of the time; with 125 parts it does so 96% of the time.
- **Drift.** When subgroup means shift randomly, Cpk, which uses within-subgroup variation, barely moves (1.28 even when the shift SD equals the within SD). Ppk falls to 0.92, close to the true long-term 0.94. The within-based ppm (142) then understates the true nonconforming rate (4678) by a factor of 30. A gap between Cpk and Ppk is the signal that the process is not in control.
- **Skewness.** For lognormal data with the USL four SDs above the mean, Ppu is 1.36 and normal theory predicts 155 ppm. The true rate is 5651 ppm, 36 times higher, because the long right tail is far heavier than the normal tail. Capability indices summarize only the first two moments.

>> Practical guidance

- Establish statistical control with a control chart first. Report Cpk and Ppk together, and treat a large gap as a stability problem, not a capability result.
- Report intervals, or at least the lower confidence bound, and require it to meet the target. With fewer than about 100 observations a point estimate near 1.33 says little.
- Check normality, with a probability plot and tests suited to tails, before converting indices to ppm. For skewed data, transform (for example Box–Cox), fit a suitable distribution, or use percentile-based indices.
- State the within-SD estimator and whether unbiasing constants were used. The choice matters little for stable data but must be consistent between studies.

>> References

- D. C. Montgomery (2019). *Introduction to Statistical Quality Control*, 8th ed. Wiley.
- V. E. Kane (1986). Process capability indices. *Journal of Quality Technology* 18(1), 41-52. doi:10.1080/00224065.1986.11978984
- A. F. Bissell (1990). How reliable is your capability index? *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society C* 39(3), 331-340. doi:10.2307/2347383
- R. A. Boyles (1991). The Taguchi capability index. *Journal of Quality Technology* 23(1), 17-26. doi:10.1080/00224065.1991.11979279
- S. Kotz and N. L. Johnson (2002). Process capability indices—a review, 1992–2000. *Journal of Quality Technology* 34(1), 2-19. doi:10.1080/00224065.2002.11980119
- L. Scrucca (2004). qcc: an R package for quality control charting and statistical process control. *R News* 4(1), 11-17.
